## 3) Ownership & Write Permissions (Who is allowed to write what)

- md/* writes: MarketDataEvent, FeatureEvent
- state/* writes: Snapshot projections only
- alpha/* writes: SignalEvent only (scores/forecasts)
- portfolio/* writes: TargetEvent, OrderIntentEvent
- risk/* writes: RiskCheckEvent (allow/deny + reasons)
- oms/* writes: all Order lifecycle events (created/ack/fill/cancel/reject)
- execution/* writes: execution logs/events (request/response/latency)
- adapters/* writes: normalized inbound venue events only (no decisions)
- posttrade/* writes: PositionUpdatedEvent, PnLEvent

If a module writes outside its ownership, it's an architecture violation.

---

## 4) The 6-Line “Whole System” Mental Model

A correct system can be summarized as:

market_event = md_adapter.recv()
snapshot     = state_registry.update(market_event)

signal       = alpha.score(snapshot)
target       = portfolio.build(signal, positions, constraints)
intent       = portfolio.to_intent(target)

result       = risk.check(intent, context)
if result.allowed:
    order = oms.create(intent)
    execution.submit(order)

Everything else exists to:
- make this deterministic
- make it safe
- make it observable
- make it replayable

---

## 5) Scaling Path (Institutional Roadmap)

Phase 1 (single Python process):
- In-proc EventBus + append-only EventStore
- Async tasks for:
  - md ingest
  - user stream
  - strategy cycle
  - reconciliation
  - event persistence

Phase 2 (service split, same boundaries):
- Replace in-proc bus with Kafka/NATS/Redpanda
- Run md/alpha/portfolio/risk/oms/execution as separate processes
- Keep contracts identical so migration is mechanical

---

## 6) Common Failure Modes (Design for them upfront)

- data stale / gaps / reconnect
- duplicate messages
- out-of-order acks/fills
- fill after cancel request
- REST snapshot differs from stream
- retries causing duplicates unless idempotent
- restart with in-flight orders

The structure above is chosen specifically to make these failures:
- detectable (events/metrics)
- containable (risk + circuit breakers)
- debuggable (replay + correlation ids)

---

## 7) Non-Negotiable Principles (Institutional Grade)

- Strategy/Alpha produces no orders.
- Risk is a hard gate before OMS submission.
- OMS is the only owner of order state.
- Adapters are IO only.
- Everything emits events; replay explains behavior.
- Default safe mode is: execution disabled.
